Apple and Ireland have won their appeal  against the European Commission over a  13 billion euro unpaid tax bill Ireland  correspondent Stephen Murphy is in  Dublin forests and Stephen a pretty  important victory for Ireland's economy  well that's a matter of debate Adam I  mean it's a victory for the Irish  government which is always denied that  it had made any favorable tax  arrangements that would favor Apple but  certainly it under surface others it  would seem pretty bizarre that any  country would try to fight against  receiving a tax windfall of 13 point 1  billion euro it's actually over 14  billion euro when you include the  interest payments but all of this dates  back to a European Commission decision  in 2016 that Ireland had favored Apple  over other companies doing business in  arms with a certain set of complicated  tax arrangements which effectively  allowed Apple to under pay its tax  obligations by as I said more than 13  billion euros around eleven point eight  billion pounds Europe then ordered our  and to recover that money from Apple but  the Irish government said it would not  do so because it had always denied that  it had favored apple in any way it was  also keen to find a perception that  Ireland could be considered some sort of  offshore tax haven so the Irish  government then underly over on for now  under Niall Martin had forced this case  to the General Court of the European  Union which this morning annulled  that original decision from 2016 that  Apple had to pay this money in that  decision today just in the in the last  half are coming from Brussels the  General Court Street that Apple had not  been granted any selective economic  advantage by Irish tax policies we found  reaction from the Irish government  straight away the Department of Finance  saying Ireland has always been clear  there was no special treatment to  provide it to Apple the correct amount  of Irish tax was charged taxation in  line with ordinary Irish taxation rules  Apple has also welcomed this news but I  mean as I alluded to there to sorry this  is not going to be a universally popular  decision against the backdrop  of an economy that has been completely  battered by the corona virus pandemic  here we have the government of the day  turning down a cash windfall from the  world's largest company of 14 billion  euro which could have been spent on  health or housing or one of the other  pressing issues this decision though it  is widely expected that the European  Commission will appeal this to the  European Court of Justice so the saga  isn't over yet that 14 billion euro is  being held in an escrow account for the  moment
 

